### Proposed defaults included in approval

- **Email:** generic IMAP + SMTP first; preserve Gmail; Graph next. IMAP credentials/app passwords are supported only where that server permits them. Do not advertise universal provider compatibility. Multiple personal mailboxes and a visible default sender are included; shared/delegated mailbox administration and POP3 are deferred. Reject duplicate active connections to the same mailbox; changing adapters requires an explicit, verified migration/reconnect. Ambiguous alias identity requires review and never triggers automatic history merging.
- **History:** proposed initial selection is Inbox and Sent for the last 90 days, editable before connection. Discover actual provider folder roles, make limits visible and support resumable batches. Existing Gmail accounts are preserved; no bulk historical re-import happens silently.
- **Local connectors:** Kavenegar first and Melipayamak second as qualification defaults, followed by as many verified SMS providers as practical at the end of P1; one qualified Asterisk-compatible PBX first. Sepidar is the proposed accounting target conditional on its actual web-service contract. No exclusivity or provider purchase is approved by this document.
- **Credentials/networking:** encrypted server-side storage, least privilege, fresh identity confirmation for sensitive changes, redacted diagnostics and per-connection generation fencing. Custom mail/PBX/accounting endpoints require safe egress controls; administrators explicitly allow legitimate internal hosts and custom CAs. Do not solve self-hosting with disabled TLS verification or unrestricted access to loopback/metadata networks.
- **Delivery:** preserve accepted, delivered, failed and unknown distinctions. Provider APIs do not imply exactly-once delivery. Unknown side effects require reconciliation or user review before retry. Connections never send a test message merely to validate credentials.
- **UI and language:** keep the current shell, navigation, typography/tokens and shared components. Every setup, consent, progress, empty, validation, timeout, quota, expiry, retry, success, disconnect and revocation state has contextual FA/DE/EN copy, accessibility labels and operator instructions. Review Farsi first, then German, with mobile, RTL/LTR, light/dark, focus and mixed-direction addresses. Preserve address/secret bytes and keep user locale separate from recipient language, country, calendar and currency.
- **Privacy and offboarding:** preserve current sharing and mailbox ownership; new mailboxes default private. Recheck authority before accessing credentials or dispatching work. Local suspension/disconnect prevents new dispatch and fences old jobs; already accepted external sends/calls may complete. Remote revocation is provider-specific; deleting an IMAP password locally cannot revoke it remotely, and any required provider-side action must be shown clearly.

### Evidence and validation per increment

Use the declared Bun runtime and lockfile. Before selecting mail/MIME/provider libraries, qualify compatibility, maintenance, licenses and advisories; do not weaken the completed auth controls or change the package manager. Required feature/bugfix tests start with a failing behavior; database coverage uses real PostgreSQL.

Acceptance combines provider contract tests with real controlled IMAP/SMTP servers and authorized provider/PBX/accounting environments. Cover restart/crash, malformed or hostile messages, HTML sanitization, attachments, timeouts, TLS failure, quota/throttling, cursor reset, duplicate callbacks, uncertain sends, offline recovery and suspension during queued/in-flight work. New providers must participate in auth revocation workers and must not resurrect credentials after removal.

Run scoped checks per increment and full relevant unit/integration, translation, typing, lint, production application and worker builds for release. Rehearse fresh/migrated installation, database/object/key restore, permission-safe exports and preserved UUIDs/preferences. Public capability claims distinguish implemented code, contract fixtures and real provider-tested operation.

### Assumptions and gates

| Assumption                                                                  | How it is resolved                                                                                                                  | Owner / gate                                       |
| --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| Independent email plus local communications is an adoption driver.          | Interview pilot teams, measure their current workflow, and observe completion/continued use.                                        | Ali and maintainers, IG-17.                        |
| Chosen mail servers/libraries support required behavior on Bun.             | Small controlled protocol qualification before committing the full adapter; document capability gaps.                               | Maintainers, IG-01 to IG-04.                       |
| SMS accounts provide the necessary lines, receipts and inbound access.      | Check the exact plan/API generation and use approved test numbers; retain polling/recovery where callbacks cannot be authenticated. | Maintainers with Ali-approved access, IG-07/IG-15. |
| Asterisk-family behavior matches the customer's installation.               | Pin and test actual PBX/version, event access and extension configuration.                                                          | Maintainers with approved PBX access, IG-10.       |
| Sepidar exposes the needed operations under an available license.           | Obtain authoritative API/edition/entitlement details and permitted test installation; agree mappings before financial writes.       | Ali and maintainers, IG-13 blocks IG-14.           |
| Rial/toman and quotation/accounting rules can be represented unambiguously. | Approve real input/output examples, units and rounding before implementing presentation or posting.                                 | Ali and maintainers, IG-06/IG-12/IG-13.            |

## 8. Release and approval

1. **First release candidate:** IG-01 through IG-08. Independent customer email, usable connection management, Iranian SMS/data conventions and full Farsi/German acceptance. Provider operation is not marked complete while its real-account qualification is unavailable.
2. **Operational tranche:** IG-09 through IG-17, respecting dependencies; broad verified SMS-provider expansion closes this tranche after the other P1 work. Qualification can proceed early; actual integration delivery remains conditional on verified provider access and behavior.
3. **Later:** IG-18 through IG-22 require a separate scope decision. Full accounting/ERP, tax filing, native mobile apps, PBX recordings, an unrestricted plugin runtime and broad competitor feature parity are not part of this approval.

Release success is measured against Section 4 and the task acceptance criteria. Each release records its exact commit, provider/version capability matrix, migration/restore evidence, UI captures and remaining installation gates. Approval does not turn the source research into verified competitor claims or imply that five pilots establish market fit.

**Approval recorded:** Ali approved P0 and P1 with these defaults on 31 October 2026 and requested a new implementation goal, beginning with non-Gmail email while preserving the current UI and fully testing Farsi/German workflows. P2 remains deferred. The original plan was prepared without implementation changes; the implementation record tracks subsequent work.

### Evidence used for planning

Source inspection is pinned to the commit above. Useful starting points: [email schema](https://github.com/KhademOHAli1/warpdrive/blob/8539fe8c4aeb0b58b6d05e629710460ef90bbed4/src/db/schema/email.ts), [Gmail initial sync](https://github.com/KhademOHAli1/warpdrive/blob/8539fe8c4aeb0b58b6d05e629710460ef90bbed4/src/features/email/sync.ts), [connection grants UI](https://github.com/KhademOHAli1/warpdrive/blob/8539fe8c4aeb0b58b6d05e629710460ef90bbed4/src/app/%28app%29/settings/connections/ConnectionsClient.tsx), [offboarding](https://github.com/KhademOHAli1/warpdrive/blob/8539fe8c4aeb0b58b6d05e629710460ef90bbed4/src/features/auth/management/offboarding.ts) and [currency semantics](https://github.com/KhademOHAli1/warpdrive/blob/8539fe8c4aeb0b58b6d05e629710460ef90bbed4/docs/multicurrency.md).

- [IMAP standard, RFC 9051](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9051.html) describes mailbox access and identities; SMTP submission is a separate operation. [RFC 8314](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8314.html) provides the email transport-security reference. Compatibility with existing IMAP4rev1 servers must also be qualified.
- [Microsoft Graph folder delta](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/message-delta?view=graph-rest-1.0) supports incremental message sync. [Graph sendMail](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/user-sendmail?view=graph-rest-1.0) documents that accepted requests do not establish completed processing or recipient delivery.
- [Kavenegar REST documentation](https://kavenegar.com/rest.html) documents sending, status lookup, receiving and callbacks. A cryptographic callback verification scheme was not established from this review; the plan treats authoritative reconciliation as necessary.
- [Melipayamak API overview](https://www.melipayamak.com/api/), [delivery status](https://www.melipayamak.com/api/getdelivery/) and [message retrieval](https://www.melipayamak.com/api/getmessages/) establish public interfaces. The newer authenticated console's precise parity still needs qualification.
- [Asterisk AMI](https://docs.asterisk.org/Configuration/Interfaces/Asterisk-Manager-Interface-AMI/AMI-v2-Specification/) and [CDR documentation](https://docs.asterisk.org/Latest_API/API_Documentation/Module_Configuration/cdr/) support the proposed PBX direction. This is not proof of compatibility with every Asterisk-based distribution.
- [Sepidar's official web-service product](https://www.sepidarsystem.com/products/web-service/) describes a separately purchased service, prerequisite systems, connector development and static-IP setup. Its [linked technical guide](https://help.sepidarsystem.com/document/introduction/) timed out during research, so the required operations, authentication and redistribution rights remain explicit qualification items.

These are public documentation checks performed 31 October 2026. They do not establish pricing, contract entitlement, customer adoption, service reliability or live integration success.
